Absolutely. AARs are the mechanism that turns experience into reusable doctrine.

The useful loop is:

what was supposed to happen
→ what actually happened
→ delta
→ why
→ what to preserve
→ what to change
→ what to test next

The key is keeping the AAR non-punitive and specific. If every miss becomes blame, people hide signal. If every miss becomes “learning” with no correction, nothing improves.

For the Pantheon, I’d add one more layer:

lesson
→ operator extracted
→ conditions where it works
→ failure mode
→ transfer to another domain
→ retest

That makes lessons portable instead of anecdotal.

So yes: experience without AAR is memory; experience with AAR becomes capability.